ÿØÿà␀JFIF␀␀H␀H␀␀ÿáåExif␀␀MM␀*␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀û␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀Ú␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀¡␀␀␀à␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀‰␀␀␀␀␀␀␀(␀␀␀␀␀␀␀1␀␀␀␀␀␀‘2␀␀␀␀␀␀¯;␀␀␀␀!␀␀Ã‚˜␀␀␀␀0␀␀ä‡i␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀@␀␀␀The Chandra X-ray image of Sirius A & B, a double star system located 8.6 light years from Earth, shows a bright source and a dim source. The central bright source is Sirius B, a dense white dwarf star with a surface temperature of about 25,000 degrees Celsius. The dim source (slightly above and to the right of Sirius B) is Sirius A, a normal star more than twice as massive as the Sun. The spoke-like pattern of light is an instrument artifact due to the transmission grating.

The white dwarf, Sirius B, has a mass equal to the mass of the Sun packed into a diameter that is 90% that of the Earth. The gravity on the surface of Sirius B is 400,000 times that of Earth!␀␀
